digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56871-1
  rankdir="TB";
  "K1" [label="(0,0,0) If a cellular stress-response pathway is important for survival under fluctuating stress, then clarifying how it senses stress and activates its regulator is a worthwhile research goal."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) If the canonical model for environmental-stress signaling requires stressosome-mediated sensing that releases RsbT, then unclear stressosome sensing/activation implies a key knowledge gap."];
  "K3" [label="(0,0,0) If a proposed sensor domain is systematically mutated and inducibility remains while response magnitude/duration changes, then that domain is unlikely to be essential for sensing and instead modulates response dynamics."];
  "K6" [label="(0,0,0) induction-common: If multiple substitutions in a proposed sensing region repeatedly change response magnitude/duration without abolishing inducibility, then generalize that the region modulates dynamics rather than sensing."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, bacteria constantly experience fluctuating natural conditions, including stress-causing conditions."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, appropriate sensing and adaptation to stressors is important for survival and growth."];
  "N24" [label="(53,0,0) Currently, experimental evidence that RsbR proteins truly sense stress has remained elusive despite well-designed studies."];
  "N25" [label="(54,0,0) Currently, this study sought experimental evidence for RsbR-mediated stress sensing using RsbRA as a model."];
  "N27" [label="(56,0,0) Currently, those substitutions altered magnitude/duration of σB response to ethanol, but none was critical for stress sensing."];
  "N29" [label="(58,0,0) Currently, stressosome-deleted cells retain an environmental stress-inducible σB response that strictly depends on RsbT."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper investigates and refines the Bacillus σB environmental-stress signaling model by testing whether RsbT can mediate stress detection and σB activation independently of RsbR-containing stressosome complexes, while the stressosome primarily modulates the magnitude and timing of the general stress response.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When mutational evidence weakens an essential RsbR/stressosome sensing role and stressosome-deleted cells retain an RsbT-dependent σB response, the σB environmental-stress model should be investigated and refined around stressosome-independent RsbT-centered sensing with stressosome modulation of response dynamics."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because bacteria experience fluctuating stress-causing conditions, stress sensing and adaptation are survival-relevant cellular functions."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Clarifying how survival-relevant bacterial stress-response pathways sense stress and activate their regulators is a worthwhile research goal."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Substitutions in a proposed sensing region that alter σB response magnitude or duration without abolishing inducibility support interpreting that region as modulating response dynamics rather than being essential for stress sensing."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) The unresolved evidence for RsbR-mediated stress sensing motivates investigating whether RsbR/stressosome-associated mechanisms actually provide environmental stress sensing."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Seeking experimental evidence for RsbR-mediated stress sensing is a justified way to clarify a survival-relevant bacterial stress-response sensing mechanism."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) If mutations in a proposed RsbR sensing region mainly alter response dynamics while the canonical stressosome-sensing model remains mechanistically unclear, then reexamining whether the stressosome is the essential environmental sensor is motivated."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) An environmental-stress-inducible σB response that remains after stressosome deletion but strictly depends on RsbT motivates considering RsbT or an RsbT-centered mechanism as the proximal mediator of that response."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) A survival-relevant bacterial stress-response pathway can be treated as a justified subject for investigating stress sensing and regulator activation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Bacteria facing fluctuating stressful environments motivate investigation of stress-response sensing and adaptation mechanisms."];
